    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 242333\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n433 PM MST Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 00z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect through Saturday with \n  daily Major to Extreme Heat Risk.\n\n- Lower desert highs are likely to reach or exceed 110 degrees\n  through the rest of the work week and even into the weekend, \n  peaking around daily records during the middle of the week.\n\n- Monsoon storm chances will be very limited this week and \n  essentially come to an end by Tuesday before slowly returning \n  this coming weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nAn expansive subtropical anticyclone can be seen in midlevel \nwater vapor imagery centered over Southern NM/far Western TX, \nresulting in southerly to southeasterly flow aloft. A pocket of \nconsiderably drier air is evident nudging into Southeastern AZ and\nwill continue to filter into the area as we head into the middle \nof the work week. Shower/thunderstorm activity from yesterday \nevening has effectively overturned the atmosphere over South- \nCentral AZ, evident in the 12Z PSR sounding which shows a nearly \nmoist adiabatic temperature profile above the boundary layer and \nonly skinny elevated CAPE between approximately 600-400 mb. \nInhibition is very unlikely to be overcome across the eastern CWA \nthis afternoon/evening, except maybe for a couple thunderstorms \nover the most prominent terrain of Southern Gila County. However, \nthe same cannot be said about portions of the western CWA. \nMarginally supportive low level moisture remains in place across \nSouthwest AZ and Southeast CA with near surface mixing ratios \nbetween 9-11 g/kg and RAP analyzed PWATs between 1.4-1.7\". While \nCIN is still high (-50 to -125 J/kg), there may be a window late \nthis afternoon where outflows from the Baja Spine could spark some\nstrong to severe storms over Imperial County. This is a very low \nprobability, but the potential is worth mentioning. Otherwise, \nanticipate 10-20% chances for isolated thunderstorms over \nprominent terrain features near JTNP, the Kofa Mountains in \nSouthwest AZ, and perhaps the Harcuvar Mountains. \n\nEnsembles remain in excellent agreement that the subtropical \nanticyclone will continue shifting westward and strengthen \ngradually heading into the middle of the work week. With the \ncenter of the anticyclone settling along the AZ/NM border, \nensemble mean H5 heights by Wednesday will reach a 595-597 dam \nrange, around the 98th-99th percentile of climatology. NAEFs mean\n850 mb temperatures respond quickly, with values peaking around \n29C-32C, near climatological maximum for late Summer. This will \nsupport lower desert afternoon highs around 10F above daily \nnormals, near record highs through the middle of the week. This \nwill follow overnight lows in the 80s to around 90F, offering \nlittle overnight relief from the heat. Extreme Heat Warnings \nremain in effect through Saturday across much of the area and will\nlikely need to be expanded to cover communities such as Globe, \nTonto Basin, and rural communities outside the typically hotter \nlower desert locales during the latter half of the week. The other\nresult of the high settling closer to the forecast area will be \nincreasing midlevel subsidence and weak, if any, midlevel flow. \nBetter quality moisture will be confined to our south, mostly over\nSonora, with 1000-700 mb mixing ratios remaining low, 6-8 g/kg \nover the forecast area. The result of this increased subsidence \nand meager moisture will be a near complete suppression of \nthunderstorm activity by Tuesday-Wednesday. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nEnsemble guidance favors some weakening of the subtropical high\nstarting Friday as it starts to shift back to the east. This shift\nassumes a Pacific trough moving into the Northwestern U.S. late\nweek. Models also favor some gradual moisture advection by Friday\nand lasting through the upcoming weekend. Given the expected \nincrease in moisture and weakening subsidence, rain chances should\nreturn as early as Friday but more likely over the weekend. \nNBM/WPC PoPs show 10-15% chances over high terrain areas Friday \nand Saturday before increasing and expanding into the lower \ndeserts by Sunday. These PoPs may be underdone somewhat \nconsidering what the latest models runs show, so we anticipate \nhigher PoPs soon from the NBM. Temperatures should also begin to \nimprove late week and through the weekend as moisture increases \nand heights gradually lower. The latest forecast highs still \nmaintain or even top 110 degrees through Saturday before dropping \nto below 110 degrees by Sunday or Monday of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2330Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nNo aviation concerns under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks can be \nexpected throughout the TAF period. The overall wind pattern will \nfollow the typical diurnal tendencies with speeds aob 10 kts. \nPeriods of light variability can be expected, especially during \ndiurnal transitions.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo aviation concerns under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks can be \nexpected throughout the TAF period. Winds will generally be out of\nthe southeast at KIPL and fluctuate between the south-southeast \nand southwest at KBLH. Overall wind speeds will generally be aob \n10 kts with some modest afternoon/early evening gusts, especially \nat KBLH.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot and seasonally dry conditions will prevail for much if not all\nof the coming week. Strong high pressure will settle over the\nregion by midweek with gradual drying conditions. Minimum RHs \ntoday will mostly fall between 20-30%, before dropping back into \nthe teens starting Tuesday. Temperatures will be around 5-10 \ndegrees above normal, with lower desert highs eclipsing 110 \ndegrees nearly every afternoon. Outside of distant thunderstorm\noutflows, expect overall light winds following typical diurnal \ntendencies with some modest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 24   115 in 2020    119 in 1985    117 in 1985\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\nAug 29   116 in 2023    117 in 1954    117 in 2005\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Saturday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Saturday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Whittock\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/Whittock \nCLIMATE...18/Kuhlman\n",
